@@ -0,0 +1,43 @@
+---
+id: dl-002-atomicity-via-immutability-not-rollback-copy-deep-clones-with-structuredclone
+type: decision-log
+title: "Atomicity via immutability, not rollback; copy deep-clones with structuredClone"
+status: pending
+---
+
+## Context
+
+`applyPatch(document, patch)` (RFC 6902) must return a new document without mutating its input,
+and must apply a patch's operations atomically: if any operation in the sequence fails (including
+a failed `test`), the caller's original `document` must be unaffected. Separately, the `copy`
+operation duplicates a value from one location in the document to another, and the two copies must
+not be able to affect each other afterwards.
+
+## Decision
+
+- Every container-level primitive (`setMember`, `insertMember`, `deleteMember`, and the `rebuildAt`
+  tree-rebuilding they're driven by) returns a new array/object instead of mutating its input, and
+  this holds transitively through each `op*` function. As a consequence, `applyPatch` never writes
+  to `document` at any point, success or failure; atomicity on a mid-patch error is a direct
+  corollary of that, not a separately implemented rollback or an upfront deep clone of the whole
+  document. `patch.reduce(...)` simply stops at the throwing operation and the exception propagates
+  — the last successfully-produced intermediate document is discarded along with everything after
+  it, and the original reference the caller holds was never touched.
+- `copy` clones the sourced value with the global `structuredClone` (available unmodified in the
+  Node version this project runs on; see dl-001) before handing it to `opAdd`, so the source and
+  destination never alias the same nested object/array.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Future operations/features that extend `applyPatch` (e.g. JSON Merge Patch, or batched/
+  transactional multi-patch APIs) should keep this invariant — no in-place mutation anywhere in the
+  call graph — rather than reach for explicit snapshot/rollback machinery; it's what makes
+  atomicity free here and should stay true as the file grows.
+- `add` and `replace` do *not* clone their `value`: it comes from outside the document (the patch
+  itself), so there is no aliasing risk symmetric to `copy`'s. If a later feature ever lets a patch
+  operation source `value` from inside the same document, revisit whether it needs the same
+  `structuredClone` treatment `copy` gets.
+- `structuredClone` throws on values it cannot clone (e.g. functions); since patch values must be
+  JSON in the first place, this is not expected to matter in practice, but it means a `copy` of a
+  non-JSON value embedded in the document (if one ever got in by other means) fails loudly rather
+  than silently aliasing.
