@@ -0,0 +1,66 @@
+---
+id: task-002-implement-json-patch-rfc-6902-applypatch
+type: task
+title: "Implement JSON Patch (RFC 6902) applyPatch"
+status: pending
+---
+
+## Request
+
+Implement JSON Patch (RFC 6902): export `applyPatch(document, patch)` from `src/index.ts`. It
+applies a JSON Patch (an array of operations — add, remove, replace, move, copy, test) to a JSON
+document and returns the resulting document as a new value, never mutating its input. It throws
+when the patch is invalid or when one of its operations fails. This builds on `resolvePointer`
+(RFC 6901) from task-001.
+
+## What I did
+
+- Extended `src/index.test.ts` first (red): the RFC 6902 §A examples (A.1–A.12, A.14–A.16) as
+  individual cases, plus edge cases the appendix doesn't cover — add/replace/remove at the root
+  pointer, replacing a nonexistent member, `move` refusing a `from` that is a proper prefix of
+  `path`, `copy` not aliasing the source value, non-mutation of the input document (including
+  when a later operation in the patch fails, so the original is left untouched), and malformed
+  patches/operations (not an array, missing `op`/`path`/`value`/`from`, unknown `op`, a `null`
+  `value` distinguished from a missing one).
+- Implemented `applyPatch` in `src/index.ts`, reusing and refactoring the RFC 6901 pointer logic
+  from task-001:
+  - Factored pointer tokenizing out of `resolvePointer` into `parsePointer`, unchanged in
+    behaviour.
+  - Added immutable container primitives — `setMember`, `insertMember`, `deleteMember` — each
+    returning a new array/object rather than mutating, with array-specific handling of `-` (append,
+    `add` only) and index bounds (insert allows index `== length`; set/remove require an existing
+    index).
+  - Added `rebuildAt`, which walks a pointer's tokens to the parent container and applies an
+    action there, recreating every ancestor on the path so the rest of the tree is shared
+    structurally and the original is never touched.
+  - Implemented each operation (`opAdd`, `opRemove`, `opReplace`, `opMove`, `opCopy`, `opTest`) in
+    terms of those primitives; `move` is literally `opAdd(opRemove(document, from), path, value)`
+    per the RFC's own definition, after checking `from` is not a proper prefix of `path`; `copy`
+    deep-clones the sourced value (via the global `structuredClone`) before adding it, so the two
+    locations never alias.
+  - Added a small recursive `deepEqual` for `test`, since JSON object-member order must not affect
+    equality but array order must.
+  - Validated patch shape at the boundary: `patch` must be an array; each operation must be an
+    object with a recognized `op` and a string `path`; `add`/`replace`/`test` require a `value`
+    member (checked via `hasOwnProperty`, so an explicit `null` is accepted and only a genuinely
+    missing member is rejected); `move`/`copy` require a string `from`.
+  - Because nothing is ever mutated in place, atomicity on a mid-patch failure falls out for free:
+    `applyPatch` never touches `document`, so on error the caller's original value is already
+    guaranteed intact without any explicit rollback or upfront cloning.
+- Confirmed red before the implementation existed, then green after (`npm test`, 42/42 passing,
+  including the original `resolvePointer` suite).
+- Type-checked `src/index.ts` in isolation against the project's strict compiler options using a
+  temporary, uncommitted `typescript` install (same approach as dl-001): clean. Found and fixed one
+  real type error in the process — `isContainer`'s `Record<string, unknown> | unknown[]` guard let
+  `deleteMember`'s object branch keep an array-shaped union after the preceding `Array.isArray`
+  check, which `tsc` flagged on `delete next[token]` (no string index signature on the array arm of
+  the union). Replaced it with a narrower `isRecord` guard (object, non-null, non-array) used
+  everywhere a plain-object branch is intended, which both reads more precisely and resolved the
+  error. Did not install `typescript`/`@types/node` or add a typecheck script, per dl-001.
+
+## Files changed
+
+- `src/index.ts`: added `applyPatch` and its supporting functions; refactored `resolvePointer`'s
+  pointer-tokenizing into `parsePointer` (no behaviour change); replaced `isContainer` with
+  `isRecord`.
+- `src/index.test.ts`: added the `applyPatch` test suite.
